### Strata API probe — Phase 3a
#
# Every endpoint, in the order it is worth trying. Start the host first:
#
#     dotnet run --project src/Strata.Host -c Release -- serve --verbose
#
# Then send these from a REST client (VS Code REST Client, Rider, Visual Studio .http).
# Everything is equally reachable with curl; the equivalents are noted where the shape is
# not obvious.
#
# Note on a throwaway HTML page instead: it would have to be loaded from file://, which is a
# different origin, so the browser would block every response unless the API sent
# Access-Control-Allow-Origin. Sending that would let any site the user happens to visit read
# their disk contents from localhost, which is a worse trade than using a REST client. The
# Angular app in Phase 3b has no such problem: it is served by this same host, same origin.

@host = http://localhost:5177

### Volumes — every volume, merged with what Strata has recorded about it.
# A volume with no baselineUtc has never been scanned and Tier 2 cannot run on it.
# attached=false means history exists for a volume that is not plugged in right now.
GET {{host}}/api/volumes

### Settings — everything the resident scanner is configured by.
GET {{host}}/api/settings

### Settings — partial update. Fields not sent are left alone.
PUT {{host}}/api/settings
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "intervalSeconds": 900,
  "eventFloorBytes": 1048576
}

### Settings — pause the scanner without stopping the process.
PUT {{host}}/api/settings
Content-Type: application/json

{ "paused": true }

### Settings — and resume.
PUT {{host}}/api/settings
Content-Type: application/json

{ "paused": false }

### Settings — rejected. An interval this short would busy-loop against the disk.
PUT {{host}}/api/settings
Content-Type: application/json

{ "intervalSeconds": 1 }

### Snapshots — newest first.
# rolledUpDirCount is the field that matters for /diff: a snapshot with zero cannot take part
# in one. Ticks taken before Phase 3a recorded none.
GET {{host}}/api/snapshots?volume=C:&limit=20

### Snapshots — a time range. A value with no zone is read as UTC.
GET {{host}}/api/snapshots?volume=C:&from=2026-08-19T00:00:00Z&to=2026-08-20T00:00:00Z

### Events — the change log the Timeline screen is built on.
GET {{host}}/api/events?limit=50

### Events — only changes above 100 MB.
GET {{host}}/api/events?minBytes=104857600&limit=50

### Tree — current state, one level under the volume root.
# Nodes carry parentId and a leaf name rather than a full path: the caller knows the path it
# asked for, and every other path is that plus the names on the way down.
GET {{host}}/api/tree?volume=C:&depth=1

### Tree — drill into a path. Backslashes, forward slashes and a bare path all work.
GET {{host}}/api/tree?volume=C:&path=C:\Users\chris\AppData\Local&depth=1

### Tree — three levels. About 9,700 nodes and 2.6 MB on the target machine.
GET {{host}}/api/tree?volume=C:&depth=3

### Tree — the pathological case: the whole volume, 571k directories, 107 MB.
# Streamed, so the first byte arrives in about 2 ms and the rest takes 3.4 s. Raising the limit
# above the 10,000 default is what makes it walk the whole tree.
GET {{host}}/api/tree?volume=C:&depth=32&limit=600000

### Tree — historical, out of one snapshot's stored rollup.
# Bounded by the depth cap and size floor that snapshot was written with, so it is not the same
# answer as the current-state walk and the API does not pretend it is.
GET {{host}}/api/tree?snapshot=1&depth=2

### Diff — what changed between two snapshots, attributed to the directories that explain it.
# Substitute two ids whose rolledUpDirCount is above zero.
GET {{host}}/api/diff?from=1&to=46

### Diff — scoped to a subtree, with a 1 GB reporting floor.
GET {{host}}/api/diff?from=1&to=46&path=C:\Users&minBytes=1073741824

### Diff — refused, with the reason, when a snapshot has no rollup to compare.
GET {{host}}/api/diff?from=1&to=2

### Scan log — every tick the scheduler attempted, with the summary for the same window.
# The one endpoint that exists to be sceptical of the scanner. Deferred rows are the point:
# a log of successes alone would be evidence of nothing.
GET {{host}}/api/scan-log?limit=50

### Scan log — only the ticks that did not run, and why.
# The summary still counts everything in the window, so "6 of 56" survives the filter.
GET {{host}}/api/scan-log?result=deferred

### Scan log — one volume, today.
GET {{host}}/api/scan-log?volume=C:&from=2026-08-19T00:00:00Z

### Scan log — rejected.
GET {{host}}/api/scan-log?result=maybe

### Scan — queue an incremental tick. Answers 202; the tick has not run yet.
POST {{host}}/api/scan
Content-Type: application/json

{ "volume": "C:", "tier": "incremental" }

### Scan — queue a full baseline. Minutes, not seconds: 211 s cold for 3M files.
POST {{host}}/api/scan
Content-Type: application/json

{ "volume": "D:", "tier": "full" }

### Scan — rejected.
POST {{host}}/api/scan
Content-Type: application/json

{ "volume": "C:", "tier": "sideways" }

### Not found — a volume that does not exist.
GET {{host}}/api/snapshots?volume=Q:

### Bad request — a time that is not a time.
GET {{host}}/api/events?from=yesterday

### Bad request — /tree needs either a volume or a snapshot.
GET {{host}}/api/tree

### Local only. This must fail to connect from any address that is not loopback.
# Confirm the listeners with:
#     Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 5177 -State Listen
# Expected: 127.0.0.1 and ::1, and nothing else.
GET http://127.0.0.1:5177/api/volumes
